\f1\fs22 Hayley was the last to retire for the night. Glad for having her own night-shirt and toiletries, she prepared for bed, feeling exhausted. But when her ear hit the pillow, her eyes started to itch and her toes felt pins-and-needles. She tossed over on her other side and pummelled another pillow. Like the first, it was too perfect for her liking. It kept whispering to her, 
\f2\i I\'92m the best pillow you\'92ll ever sleep on
\f1\i0 . The blanket snuggled her just a tad too snuggly, so she had to toss it off. Then the air-conditioning felt a tad too cold, so she grabbed for its remote and knocked her water glass off the bedside table. She got a towel from the bathroom and mopped up the excess water. She turned off the aircon. She tried to settle down again but, after the soft hum of the aircon, all she could hear were crickets. No, not nothing. Actually crickets. Maybe they weren\'92t all that loud, but she was so unaccustomed to sleeping in anything but a tower above the Layer, the randy little insects were sending her orthogonally crazy. The air grew warmer and she began to sweat and imagine the sheets spoke to her saying, 
\f2\i That\'92s okay, we\'92re the finest sheets you\'92ll ever sleep between.
\f1\i0 \
