\cf2 Hayley jumped to the floor. \'93That\'92s it,\'94 she said out loud. \'93No more talking bed linen.\'94 She grabbed her tablet and stormed down to the kitchen, where she poured the last glass of the red Kathy had opened.\
Truth was, her itching eyes, tingly feet, icy aircon, and chatty bed linen were all emissaries of a thought stuck under the floorboards of her mind. 
\f2\i Be curious
\f1\i0 , they were telling her. Curious she was now, as she sipped the cabernet and tried to relax.\
It was something Kathy said. Or did. Or almost did. On impulse, she grabbed her tablet and searched for stasis fridge maintenance call-outs. She frowned as she scrolled to the end of the list much quicker than she expected.\
\'93Is this all there are? In the whole country?\'94\
She sorted the list by reverse date and ran her finger down the screen to the date of Travis Teh\'92s murder.\
\'93Oh, my holy tax returns,\'94 she blurted.\
There was only one entry, and it was on the same floor in Travis Teh\'92s apartment block. In the 
\fs18 RESIDENT 
\fs22 column was a single word: 
\f2\i Chuglingham
\f1\i0 .\
By the time Hayley had her next cogent thought, she was in the stasis closet, standing with a butter knife taken from a kitchen drawer, before the panel marked 
\fs18 NO SERVICEABLE PARTS INSIDE
\fs22  that she\'92d restrained Kathy from touching only hours earlier. And it wasn\'92t even a good cogent thought; it went something like, 
\f2\i Surely Fridge Force wouldn\'92t dare invade Eloise\'92s home\'85
\f1\i0 \
That should have been a warning.\
There were no screws fastening the panel to the wall. As far as Hayley could tell, it clung like a lid on a jam jar. A quick twist with a knife should prise it off. Easy. Those Fridge Force guys were too confident in the menace of their authority, as if it was all the security their proprietary secrets required.\
That should have been a warning.\
Hayley slid the knife between panel and wall and twisted. The panel popped off like a jam jar lid and clanged to the floor. Hayley froze and held her breath, listening for movement upstairs. When none came, she peered into the cavity behind the wall and saw\'85 what? She recognised nothing, but visions came to mind of cramming a car engine and the contents of her kitchen utensils and saucepans drawer in there.\
There was a bulbous green component which bore a sign\'97\
\
